{
  "title": "Super-Human Performance in Gran Turismo Sport Using Deep Reinforcement Learning",
  "authors": [
    "Florian Fuchs",
    "Yunlong Song",
    "Elia Kaufmann",
    "Davide Scaramuzza",
    "Peter Du\u0308rr"
  ],
  "year": 2021,
  "arxivId": "2008.07971",
  "abstract": "This paper presents a learning-based system for autonomous car racing in Gran Turismo Sport, a world-leading car simulator. The system uses deep reinforcement learning and a course-progress proxy reward to train a neural network policy for vehicle control. The approach achieves super-human performance, outperforming the fastest human driver in a dataset of over 50,000 human players and the built-in AI.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "The Core Idea",
      "content": "The goal of autonomous car racing is to complete a given track as fast as possible. <strong>Classical approaches</strong> decouple the racing problem into trajectory planning and vehicle control. However, these approaches have limitations, such as the requirement of expensive computations for nonlinear optimization and the lack of flexibility in the objective. <strong>Deep reinforcement learning</strong> is a promising approach for controlling the vehicle at high speed.",
